# PicoClaw on foxbridge

PicoClaw is a lightweight AI agent that you talk to over Telegram. It relays
your messages to an LLM provider (Claude, OpenAI, Gemini, etc.) and can
optionally call back into a small set of safe tools (web search, web fetch,
cron scheduling, read-only file access).

This install runs upstream binary v0.2.6 as a systemd service
(`picoclaw.service`), pinned to armv7.

## Upstream warning

Sipeed's README says: "PicoClaw is in early rapid development. There may be
unresolved security issues. Do not deploy to production before v1.0."
Current release is 0.2.6. Treat this as a beta feature on the foxbridge.

## First-time setup

1.  In Telegram, talk to `@BotFather` → `/newbot` → pick a name → save the
    token it returns.
2.  Talk to `@userinfobot` → note your numeric user ID.
3.  Edit `/etc/default/picoclaw` (as root):
    - Replace `CHANGEME_TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN` with the BotFather token.
    - Replace `CHANGEME_ANTHROPIC_API_KEY` with your Anthropic API key.
4.  Edit `/etc/picoclaw/config.json.tmpl`:
    - Under `channels.telegram.allow_from`, change `[]` to `[123456789]`
      (your numeric user ID). Add more IDs if other people should be
      allowed.
5.  `systemctl restart picoclaw`
6.  `journalctl -u picoclaw -f` to watch it come up.
7.  Message your bot in Telegram. First reply takes 2–5 seconds.

## Enable / disable the service

-   Start now:     `systemctl start picoclaw`
-   Stop now:      `systemctl stop picoclaw`
-   Enable on boot:  `systemctl enable picoclaw`   (default — already on)
-   Disable on boot: `systemctl disable picoclaw`
-   Fully remove:  remove `picoclaw` from `IMAGE_INSTALL` in
    `meta-luckfox-distro/recipes-core/images/luckfox-image-minimal.inc`
    and rebuild — or just `systemctl disable --now picoclaw`.

The service refuses to start while any `CHANGEME_` markers remain, so a
freshly-flashed image boots cleanly without spurious failures. Check status
with `systemctl status picoclaw`.

## Switching LLM providers

PicoClaw's `model_list` in `/etc/picoclaw/config.json.tmpl` defaults to
Anthropic Claude. To switch, replace that entry with one of the snippets
below, and set the matching `${...}_API_KEY` in `/etc/default/picoclaw`.

### OpenAI

```json
"model_list": [
  { "model_name": "gpt", "model": "openai/gpt-4o-mini",
    "api_keys": ["${OPENAI_API_KEY}"] }
]
```
Then set `agents.defaults.model_name` to `"gpt"` and uncomment
`OPENAI_API_KEY=` in `/etc/default/picoclaw`.

Note: the `<vendor>/<model>` prefix is mandatory. Without it picoclaw
falls back to the OpenAI protocol and will send whatever key you
configured to `api.openai.com`, regardless of the actual provider —
which produces a misleading "Incorrect API key" error.

### Google Gemini

```json
{ "model_name": "gemini", "model": "gemini/gemini-1.5-flash",
  "api_keys": ["${GOOGLE_API_KEY}"] }
```

### DeepSeek

```json
{ "model_name": "deepseek", "model": "deepseek/deepseek-chat",
  "api_keys": ["${DEEPSEEK_API_KEY}"] }
```

### Groq

```json
{ "model_name": "groq", "model": "groq/llama-3.3-70b-versatile",
  "api_keys": ["${GROQ_API_KEY}"] }
```

### Ollama (local, off-device)

Point picoclaw at an Ollama server running on another machine on your LAN:

```json
{ "model_name": "local", "model": "ollama/llama3.2",
  "api_base": "http://192.168.1.10:11434/v1", "api_keys": [""] }
```

Don't try to run Ollama on the foxbridge itself — 512 MB DDR3L cannot
hold a useful local model.

## Tool permissions (read-only-ish default)

By default, the following tools are **disabled** because they're dangerous
when the agent is driven by a Telegram chat that could be compromised:

-   `exec`          — shell command execution
-   `write_file`, `append_file`, `edit_file`  — filesystem writes
-   `spawn`, `subagent`  — spawning sub-processes / sub-agents
-   `install_skill` — installing third-party skills (arbitrary code)
-   `i2c`, `spi`    — direct hardware bus access
-   `mcp`           — Model Context Protocol servers

To enable any of them, set `"enabled": true` in the matching block in
`/etc/picoclaw/config.json.tmpl` and `systemctl restart picoclaw`.
**Only do this if you understand the risk** — an attacker with the bot
token or a compromised allowed user could use these tools to trash the box.

Enabled by default (safe-ish):

-   `web` + `web_fetch` — HTTP fetch for the agent to read the open web
-   `read_file`, `list_dir`  — read-only filesystem (`mode: safe`)
-   `cron`          — schedule prompts
-   `message`, `find_skills`, `skills` — agent messaging / skill discovery

## Where things live

| Path | Purpose | Persisted? |
|------|---------|------------|
| `/usr/bin/picoclaw` | Main binary (28 MB) | image |
| `/usr/bin/picoclaw-launcher[-tui]` | Interactive setup / TUI helpers | image |
| `/usr/libexec/picoclaw-start` | CHANGEME guard + env substitution | image |
| `/etc/picoclaw/config.json.tmpl` | Config template (no secrets) | eMMC |
| `/etc/picoclaw/README.md` | This file | eMMC |
| `/etc/default/picoclaw` | Secrets (mode 0640 root:picoclaw) | eMMC |
| `/var/lib/picoclaw/workspace/` | Agent state, memory, sessions | eMMC |
| `/run/picoclaw/config.json` | Rendered config with real secrets | tmpfs — wiped on reboot |

## Troubleshooting

-   `systemctl status picoclaw` — shows the CHANGEME guard message if
    placeholders remain.
-   `journalctl -u picoclaw -n 100` — picoclaw's own logs.
-   `curl -s https://api.telegram.org/bot$TELEGRAM_BOT_TOKEN/getMe` on the
    box confirms the token is valid and outbound network works.
-   Empty `allow_from` is a hard block — the guard refuses to start. This
    is intentional; a bot open to the world is dangerous.
